## Option A: select by expected role, authenticate on an existing read

Preserve the exact selected root references and the existing 64-root,
full-reference, alias, local-root, cohort, and repository checks. Carry a
separate archive-only expected-role record for the one Directory Rules
slot. Keep actual CheckedSequence values for roots whose births the pass
already reads. A role record is visibly separate from those values.

The Rules slot record comes only from the actual bounded Directory query
and the checks in steps 1–3. A bare explicit extra replayScopes ref has no
such record. The library does not infer a role from a digest, manifest row,
caller label, or private caller-record option.

When an existing root/source reader reads Rules genesis, it checks the
actual canonical full sequence association and expected Rules kind before
using those bytes. Existing finite pin/cohort, creator, repository, archive
privacy, and Replay judgment checks still apply. A role record never skips
those checks. If no existing read reaches Rules, the record remains only
selection metadata and supplies no kind proof.

This option adds no Native RPC, source open, new cache, or read deadline.
It needs an explicit planner ruling because root publication currently
uses `kind` outside the read-dependent judgment sites in 429 item 2.
It preserves the old selection intent, not an old proof guarantee: the old
ref kind was itself sender-authored metadata.

Privacy consequence: marking a ref as a replay root prevents that exact
copy from becoming a private caller anchor. It does not authorize reading
or exporting the source journal. It must not cause a private source open
just to validate the selection. If an actual read later contradicts the
expected role, that read cannot supply proof or publish private bytes.
The exact existing failure classification remains a source-integration
obligation, not a new permission assumed by this question.

## Option B: require actual Rules kind before selecting the root

Require an actual canonical Rules genesis association for every selected
root. Preserve all other closure and cohort checks. Room-mode discovery
must then add the Rules birth read that it currently omits, or return an
unavailable result when no existing read supplied it. The latter changes
availability for a currently accepted lane archive path.

A new birth read would use the existing Native birth port and archive
budget. Its present per-read reservation is one node, one call, 262,144
metadata bytes, and 2,097,152 input-work units; actual reply bytes settle
that reservation. It shares the remaining pass deadline. Those numbers
describe the existing read helper, not authorization to add its call.
This option changes read scope/work and the deliberate room-mode promise.
It must not enumerate or export a Rules journal merely to discover kind.

A separate “Directory/cohort proves actual Rules kind” option is not valid
without new protocol authority. The expected slot and actual target kind
are different facts. No proposed variant converts that slot to
CheckedSequence or skips the existing finite cohort checks.

## Persistence and ownership after either choice

Keep `Mark.replayScopes` as exact full-reference selection metadata. Do not
persist a manufactured checked context or backfill kind from old rows.
Split shape-only row decoding from the chosen live classification check.
Fresh planning and pending rebuild must receive a pass-owned context from
the same already-paid target/source preparation. Rebuild must still compare
the resulting exact ordered closure with the stored Mark before completion.
Failure to establish a required context stops the pass through its existing
unavailable path. No automatic fallback or uncharged source lookup follows.

OPS2 owns archive-build/publication/input/source-pin migration. The context
producers and rebuild plumbing also touch archive-target.ts,
archive-driver.ts, apps/room/archive.ts, and possibly archive-row.ts.
Those additional edits need root assignment after this ruling. Core Bytes
and Derive, Replay assumption records, and all protected lifecycle code
remain outside this question.

